# What a prune script would DELETE, measured without deleting anything.
#
#   build-release/bin/zelph < dev_scripts/measure-prune-reach.zph
#
# The prune of the full dump is a multi-day operation whose length is not
# known until it is over: a line's cost is its instance count times the facts
# each instance carries, and neither is written down anywhere. On 14 August
# 2026 one line -- the academic-publishing class -- turned out to hold
# 45 415 649 nodes at ~16 facts apiece, three and a half days on its own,
# after the run had already been going for fifteen hours. This script is what
# should have been run first.
#
# It reads and reports; it removes nothing, and it saves nothing.
#
# Most prune lines select by P31, so ONE pass over the P31
# facts answers all of them -- .list-predicate-value-usage sorts the classes
# by how many instances they have. The seven lines that do not are counted
# individually below. Doing it the other way round, a zelph/sources walk per
# class, would be 174 passes over the same data.
#
# What to do with the output:
#
#   * the class list of a prune script, ordered by the count this prints,
#     is the order the prune should run in -- smallest first, so the graph
#     shrinks before the expensive lines start;
#   * a class whose count is in the tens of millions is a DECISION, not a
#     line: it costs a day or more, and whether that domain belongs in the
#     artifact at all is worth asking before paying for it;
#   * the counts also belong in the header of the prune script, which
#     currently carries figures measured against a different network.
#
# Cost: one load of the 88 GB dump (~35 min, ~200 GiB of swap -- run it with
# /home/stefan/bin/restrict-process.sh, as for the prune itself), then minutes.

.lang wikidata
.load /home/stefan/zelph/wikidata-20260309-all.bin

# Every class the script prunes by P31, and every other class besides -- 400
# is comfortably more than the 174 lines, so nothing that matters is cut off.
.list-predicate-value-usage P31 400

# The four prune-facts lines (P1433, P921, P131, P195) delete facts rather
# than nodes, so their cost is the predicate's fact count -- which this same
# family of commands reports for every predicate at once.
.list-predicate-usage 300

# The three prune-nodes lines that do not select by P31.
%(each [p o] [["P6259" "Q1264450"] ["P407" "Q7850"] ["P407" "Q7737"]]
   (zelph/out (string "prune-nodes A " p " " o " -> "
                      (length (zelph/sources (zelph/resolve p "wikidata")
                                             (zelph/resolve o "wikidata")))
                      " nodes")))

.stat
.quit
